<binaryimage arch="AARCH64:LE:64:v8A:default">
<!--
    RE-friction round 1, challenge 68149b8a8f555589f353117c (Mach-O arm64
    `practice_2_rengr`): `kuna decompile ... _main` rendered
    `v2 = scanf("%d");` with the destination pointer dropped, then compared a
    local that nothing ever wrote, plus three `printf("... %s\n")` calls with no
    argument for the %s.

    Root cause (P4, active-input trial scoring), which is NOT the trial scoring
    itself.  Apple's arm64 ABI passes variadic arguments on the STACK, so clang
    emits

        add  x8, sp, #0x1c        ; &local
        mov  x9, sp
        str  x8, [x9]             ; the variadic argument, at [sp+0]
        adr  x0, "%d"             ; the one fixed parameter
        bl   scanf

    checkInputTrialUse scores the [sp+0] trial ACTIVE (ancestor realism and the
    local alias test both pass).  ParamListStandard::fillinMap then deactivates
    it again: on AArch64 x0..x7 and the outgoing stack area are ONE resource
    section, and its two positional rules, forceNoUse (a whole
    definitely-not-used exclusion group ends the section) and forceInactiveChain
    (a run of more than 2 unused slots ends the section), both read the seven
    structurally empty registers x1..x7 between the fixed parameter and the
    first vararg as evidence that the recovery has run past the argument list.
    So the argument is dropped and, with its only consumer gone, the store that
    computed it is dead code eliminated.

    Stage / sub-stage: P4 "active-input-trial-scoring".  The P0 assertion
    `option varargstackargs on|off` decides whether a VARIADIC call's stack tail
    is scored as its own fillinMap section, so the ABI's register hole stops
    being evidence about the stack argument.  Non-variadic calls are untouched:
    for a fully known prototype a hole in the register file IS evidence.

    The fixture is the same construct, hand assembled: `ask` passes the address
    of a local as the one variadic argument of `myscanf`, whose `...` prototype
    `parse line` supplies, then reads the local back.

    Pass 1 (`option varargstackargs off`) reproduces the BUG: the call keeps
    only the format string and `ask` returns a local nothing wrote.
    Pass 2 (`option varargstackargs on`) is the FIX: the stack argument is
    recovered and the local is the scanf destination.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000">
ff8300d1e9030091e8730091280100f9c000001004000094e01f40b9ff830091
c0035fd6c0035fd625640000
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="ask"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100024" name="myscanf"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>parse line extern int4 myscanf(char *fmt, ...);</com>
  <com>option varargstackargs off</com>
  <com>lo fu ask</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option varargstackargs on</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="varargstackargs #1: option off drops the stack-passed variadic argument (bug reproduced)" min="1" max="1">myscanf\(\(char \*\)0x100028\);</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="varargstackargs #2: option on recovers the variadic argument the ABI passes on the stack" min="1" max="1">myscanf\(\(char \*\)0x100028,&amp;v1\);</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
